{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>By this you shall know, etc., that it was not from my own heart.</b> One must understand how Moshe could suspect the people of HaShem — that they had not yet believed in him as a true prophet? Further, why did he repeat, saying <b>not from my own heart</b>? And perhaps it is because the ground for suspicion lay along this line: since Moshe found favor in the eyes of HaShem, when Moshe wished to confer greatness upon one of his relatives — such as the priesthood to Aharon or the princely office to Elitzafan and the like — he would act and consult HaShem, and HaShem would agree at his hand; or he would have a desire and longing in his heart to give to his brother and the like, and HaShem would grant him the wish of his heart — but not because there was any impediment, for that person was in fact the one fit for the priesthood. Therefore he said, <b>By this you shall know that HaShem sent me to do</b> etc. — not that I would act and He would agree at my hand to whatever I desired in my own mind. And corresponding to the second suspicion — that he had a longing in his heart for the matter — he said <b>that it was not from my own heart</b>, meaning: it was not that I myself desired the matter and HaShem agreed to what was in my heart. And the vocalization of the word <b>ki</b> follows along this line: the reason I do not need to prove that HaShem sent me is to exclude that I do what HaShem did not agree to, but rather to exclude that the matter was not from my own heart, as explained; and understand." "<b>If like the death of all men.</b> Meaning, along the lines of what they said concerning the verse ״אֲשֶׁר יַעֲשֶׂה אוֹתָם הָאָדָם״ — even a gentile (Bava Kamma 38a); and here the intention is to say that even in the death of the nations, which is a repugnant death, this is equivalent to death itself. And concerning the body he said <b>and be visited upon like all men</b> etc., along the lines of what is said (II Kings 9:34) ״פִּקְדוּ אֶת הָאֲרוּרָה וְקִבְרוּהָ״ — burial in the manner of all the earth. <b>Not HaShem sent me</b>, meaning — but rather from my own heart, and HaShem did not object to me. <b>And if a creation</b> etc. — for through this they would have an evil death and would lose body as well as soul."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:34.456462447Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}